{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1520615-question-about-vampires-and-bullet-wounds","id":1520615,"title":"Question about vampires and bullet wounds...","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-08-14T15:06:23-07:00","id":1520615,"title":"Question about vampires and bullet wounds...","content":"I'm not sure where to post this question, since I play in a WtA game where the storyteller has built vampire NPCs using the VtM rules, with the goal of creating a fully crossed over 20th Anniversary setting. My question is about vampires and bullet wounds. I hear that the VtM rules as written declare that ALL vampires have a peculiar resistance to being shot, converting the damage from lethal to bashing, halving it, and then rolling to soak whatever remains. I don't own any VtM books, so I can't answer this question by simply looking it up: <i>Why do vampires specifically get resistance to bullet wounds, but not to any other kind of piercing damage?</i><br /> <br /> I don't have a problem with the undead having some resistance to piercing damage, but this much resistance to ALL gunfire, without any similar resistance to other kinds of piercing damage, and without first learning a discipline that improves your soak roll, seems like an awfully strange, kludgy mechanic. it's a bit of a hot take, but I put the apparent absurdity of that rule to my fellow players and storyteller like this: a werewolf in crinos form, wielding a minigun like Jesse Ventura's character in Predator, is only going to do bashing damage to a vampire, and the vampire automatically soaks at least half of it, probably more; meanwhile, a human shivs a vampire with a small, mundane pocket knife, and the vampire has to soak it as lethal damage, with no automatic damage reduction. Is that really how the rules-as-written work? Is there some kind of lore in VtM that explains it?\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13713-wilson","name":"Wilson","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"182"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-14T17:04:32-07:00","id":1520632,"title":"","content":"Because that would make it even harder to stake them presumably. Also, if it's just playing to tropes, vampires have a history of ignoring bullets but not being cut, as Dracula in the novel met his end at the hands of a Texan with a Bowie knife. There's also something about the &quot;corpse doesn't suffer from shock&quot; excuse, where the undead body lacks some more vulnerable human bits that guns much more noticeably exploit.<br /> <br /> A minigun is probably on a different scale, though; support weapons and legit heavy, vehicle-grade weapons don't really care about the weak points of the human body, as they turn anything not made of metal into a pink mist. Also, the Garou would need to bind some kind of electricity-spirit into that or find some other way to power the firing mechanism, which is an electrically-driven motor with a surprising amount of power required (the minigun on set was plugged in, but this was cunningly concealed with camera work, mostly).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-14T20:50:04-07:00","id":1520643,"title":"","content":"So, it's worth separating things a bit here:<br /> <br /> Vampires are very resilient against any bashing damage. The halving damage before soaking it and all that applies to any bashing damage.<br /> <br /> Vampires also have a special rule that guns do bashing damage to them.<br /> <br /> That said, this is not a universal magical effect. The books allow ST discretion for guns of significant power, or vampires in particularly compromised situations, still face lethal damage. Vampires might be highly resilient to normal 9mm pistols, but you can rule that a vampire that's being held down, and has a 9mm unloaded into the back of their head at close range loses that protection. Or being shot at with large rifles such as elephant guns or anti-material rifles.<br /> <br /> That said, designers do not entirely disagree with your frustration about how bullets are treated as special in all this. This effect was expanded significantly in both Vampire: the Requiem 2e, and Vampire: the Masquerade 5e.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-15T17:34:58-07:00","id":1520726,"title":"","content":"Okay, so it's true that vampires get that oddly specific, special rule about guns, which happens to play right into their resistance to bashing damage. Is it just supposed to be one of their unexplainable supernatural traits, like how sunlight burns them, or do y'all think the game designers were trying for some kind of reasoning from physics and biology?<br /> <br /> If it's just supposed to be one of their magical traits, how do the rules-as-written define &quot;guns&quot;? If it's supposed to be some kind of reasoning from physics and biology, let's consider that there are SO many kinds of guns and ammunition in the world, from rifles to blow guns to shotguns. And just among pistols and rifles, everybody knows there's a huge difference between the kind of hole that .22 LR FMJ makes and the kind of hole that 7.62 hollow point makes. Considering shotguns, the kind of hole(s) they make is heavily dependent on the range as well as the ammunition, from a 12 gauge pistol firing buck shot to a 12 gauge with a long barrel firing slugs. If nothing else, the larger ammunition can shatter bones, which I can't believe is merely bashing damage. So which of these kinds of ammunition are reduced to bashing damage by this very special rule? My guess is that, in the 20th Anniversary Edition, which is the edition I play in and care about, all of these do bashing damage to vampires, regardless of the energy and physics involved. If it's up to the storyteller's discretion, a player's mileage will vary VASTLY.<br /> <br /> I really, really want to be hard on the designers for the sloppy way this rule seems to have be written, but I realize it wouldn't be helpful, and if I asked them directly, they might just say &quot;your storyteller shouldn't have put VtM vampires in your WtA setting in the first place.&quot; If the answer is just &quot;it's up to the storyteller&quot; when the very special gun rule for vampires applies and doesn't, I'll have to go ask my storyteller what his opinion is. Do the rules specifically advise the storyteller to use discretion?\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13713,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13713-wilson","user":"Wilson","date":". 08-15-2023, 08:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13713-wilson","name":"Wilson","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"182"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-15T21:24:14-07:00","id":1520743,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1520615-question-about-vampires-and-bullet-wounds?p=1520643#post1520643\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, it's worth separating things a bit here:<br /> <br /> Vampires are very resilient against any bashing damage. The halving damage before soaking it and all that applies to any bashing damage.<br /> <br /> Vampires also have a special rule that guns do bashing damage to them.<br /> <br /> That said, this is not a universal magical effect. The books allow ST discretion for guns of significant power, or vampires in particularly compromised situations, still face lethal damage. Vampires might be highly resilient to normal 9mm pistols, but you can rule that a vampire that's being held down, and has a 9mm unloaded into the back of their head at close range loses that protection.</div> </div> </div> </div>Technically the soak comes first, then any damage remaining is halved. At any rate, don't get in a firefight or a boxing match with a vampire.<br /> <br /> Head shots are also exempted from the bashing damage effect in the rules as written; they do lethal damage whether it's a vampire or mortal being shot (though vampires can still use their Stamina to soak, unlike living human beings). I think the Storyteller's discretion comes in as other types of blunt force trauma can be considered lethal if targeted at a particularly vital area.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}